    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d653">

                <pb facs="0712" n="454" xml:id="pg454"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1905</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-06-25T16:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 25, 1915, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 26, 4.30 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2494. The suspension of the newspaper reported in my 2469,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d653-f1"/> has been severely criticized and the paper is now
                    permitted to appear again. The gagging of the press is resented everywhere.
                    Conservative and clerical papers side with the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tageszeitung</hi> and deprecate the publication of evidently inspired
                    articles like that in <hi rendition="#italic">Lokal-Anzeiger</hi>, referred to
                    in my 2426<anchor n="2" xml:id="d653-f2"/> and 2451,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d653-f3"/> maintaining that this is a falsification of public
                    opinion in Germany which does not want war with America but will not approve of
                    any understanding with America at the cost of any concessions which would impair
                    the efficiency of the submarines. The impression seems to prevail that the
                    conciliatory articles originated in official circles and it is feared that they
                    will weaken the position of the German negotiations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
